'' Control flow: SELECT CASE and GOTO.
''
'' SELECT CASE lowers to a COMPARISON CHAIN rather than a switch. docs/plan/09 section 4
'' also describes a dense-switch and a jump-table form; the chain is the one that is
'' correct for every shape, including the ranged and relational cases a switch cannot
'' express, so it is what exists and the denser forms are an optimisation over it.
''
'' The GOTO rule was MEASURED, not assumed: tests/semantic/cleanup/trace-goto.fbc-trace
'' is fbc's own output for the same shape and shows a scope-crossing jump running the
'' cleanups of every scope it leaves, innermost first, BEFORE the branch.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/control.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 scope imbalance

'' The subject is evaluated ONCE into a slot. Re-reading it per case would call a
'' function once per branch tested, which is observable whenever it has side effects.
'' EXPECT-OUT: store @$tmp2, %4

'' 'case 4 to 6' is inclusive at both ends, so it is two comparisons and an and.
'' EXPECT-OUT: icmp.sge
'' EXPECT-OUT: icmp.sle
'' 'case is > 10' keeps its own relation rather than becoming an equality.
'' EXPECT-OUT: icmp.sgt

'' The jump out of the scope releases the scope's string first. If this line is absent
'' the string leaks on that path only, which no other assertion here would catch.
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl void fb_StrDelete(@s)

'' The pre-scan that gives a forward goto its target depth must agree with the depth
'' actually open at the label; this is what says it did.
'' EXPECT-NOT: LABEL DEPTH MISMATCH
'' EXPECT-NOT: <NO TERMINATOR>
'' GOLDEN: expected/control.out

'' A duplicate label in one procedure is refused before lowering now (TKO0600, fbc's 4) --
'' see src/control.bas's Dup. branch-cross-select-case.bas's two 'label1's, which
'' found the lowering's duplicate handling, are in two TEST subs.

'' END is a direct fb_End call. The exit code is the argument; a bare END passes 0.
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl void fb_End
'' The block cannot fall through -- fb_End does not return.
'' EXPECT-OUT: unreachable
'' And NO cleanup precedes it: the fb_StrDelete belongs to the normal scope exit, which
'' END is not. If END ran cleanups this would be a jump to the exit path instead.
'' EXPECT-OUT: end.after

'' The qualified CALL must emit, mangled through the namespace.
'' EXPECT-OUT: call void _ZN3NSQ5GREETEi
'' And the qualified ASSIGNMENT must emit a STORE. Resolving the name without giving it
'' an address made this statement silently emit nothing, which is worse than the decline
'' it replaced: it stopped being counted while still not being done.
'' EXPECT-OUT: store @@counter
